E[8] = """Document no. 685

Przemysł the Second, Duke of Poland, 29 September 1292, at Kalisz; ratifies the exchange, made five years before, of the estate of Wojsław son of Gosław called Niałek for the estates of Wisław son of Gerlach called Wrąbczyn and Bieganin.

Original on parchment. On threads of red silk hangs the seal of Duke Przemysł (seal XXVI).

Warsaw. Private library, Diplomata. Poznań, Royal State Archive. Ląd, Liber privilegiorum B, no. 22.

In the name of the Lord, amen. We wisely meet many inconveniences when we strengthen the business of our age with the protection of letters and witnesses. We therefore, Przemysł the Second, by the grace of God Duke of Poland, make known to all men, of the present age as well as of the age to come, that, being present before us and our barons, Count Wisław, son of the late Gerloch, on the one part, and Wojsław, son of Gosław, on the other, made between them this exchange of their own estates: that the said Wisław, receiving the estate of the Wojsław named above, called in the common tongue Niałek, gave and resigned in exchange two estates of his own, Wrąbczyn, which he had exchanged with Przybysław son of Lutogniew, and Bieganin, to be possessed by perpetual hereditary right. And we, considering these exchanges duly and reasonably made, approving and ratifying them, have decreed that they shall be valid for ever, all hindrance set aside. And of the fullness of our power we have freed and exempted the said villages of Wrąbczyn and Bieganin from every Polish exaction, and make them free as they were of old, namely from the naraz, from carting, from conveyance, from watch, from the podworowe, from the ox, from the cow, from the opole, from summons to the castle. And if a homicide is committed among the inhabitants of the said villages, the said Wojsław and his posterity shall take the capital penalty. But if the homicide is between one of his inhabitants and a stranger, he shall nonetheless take the penalty for homicide after his inhabitant (sic). The inhabitants of the said villages shall be summoned nowhere but before us, by letter, with our seal or ring, and shall be bound to answer there to those who complain of them. In witness of which we ordered Jaśko our notary to write the present letters, strengthening them with the protection of our seal, in the presence of these witnesses: the counts Piotrko, castellan of Poznań, Zbylut (sic), castellan of Nakło, Wit, cupbearer of Gniezno. Given at Kalisz on the feast of the blessed Michael, when we went out on campaign to Sieradz against the King of Bohemia, and then this same exchange was renewed and confirmed, which had been done and made five years before. In the year of the grace of the Lord 1292.

Nalcho, Niałek. Wroncino, Wronczyn. Beganino, Bieganin. Nakel, Nakło. Syracz, Sieradz."""

E[9] = """Document no. 1252

Dobiesław, judge of the lands of Gniezno and Kalisz, 13 November 1346, at Pyzdry; adjudges to the church of Gniezno the estates of Stropiszyn and Pępocin against the claims of the brothers Jan and Przedpełk, heirs of Trąbczyn, and the estate of Kokanin against the suit of Pietrasz, heir of Rzegocin.

Gniezno. Archive of the Metropolitan Chapter. Liber privilegiorum of the 15th century, fol. 93v.

We, Dobiesław, judge of the lands of Gniezno and Kalisz, make known to all and acknowledge to those present that, concerning the estates of Stropiszyn and Panpicza, in the presence of the noble men Mikołaj, captain of Poland, Maćko of Poznań and Mikołaj of Kalisz, palatines, and of the other witnesses written below, we absolve by the tenor of these presents Lord Kiełcz the custodian and the church of Gniezno from the claim and suit of the brothers Jan and Przedpełk, heirs of Trąbczyn; and likewise the venerable father in Christ and reverend lord, Lord Jarosław, Archbishop of the holy church of Gniezno, from the suit and claim of Pietrasz, heir of Rzegocin, for the estate of Kokanin, adjudging the said estates to them and imposing perpetual silence on the said heirs concerning them; in witness of which our seal is hung on these presents. Done and given at Pyzdry on the feast of St Brice, in the year of the Lord one thousand three hundred and forty-six, in the presence of the noble men, the palatines Maćko of Poznań and Mikołaj of Kalisz; the castellans Tomisław of Poznań, Zbylut of Gniezno and Janusz of Kalisz, and others worthy of trust.

Tropessino, Stropiszyn. Panpicza, below with the addition: alias Kopydlowo, perhaps Pępocin, or else Kopydłowo. Trampczino, Trąbczyn. Stregoczino, Rzegocin. Cokanino, Kokanin."""

E[10] = """Document no. 1253

Dobiesław, judge of the lands of Gniezno and Kalisz, 13 November 1346, at Pyzdry; adjudges to the church of Gniezno the estates of Stropiszyn and Pępocin against the claims of Andrzej, heir of Trąbczyn, and his kin.

Gniezno. Archive of the Metropolitan Chapter. Liber privilegiorum of the 15th century, fol. 93v.

We, Dobiesław, judge of the lands of Gniezno and Kalisz, make known to all and acknowledge by these presents that at Pyzdry on the feast of St Brice, in the year of the Lord 1346, in the presence of Mikołaj, captain of Poland, Maćko of Poznań and Mikołaj of Kalisz, palatines, Tomisław of Poznań, Zbylut of Gniezno and Janusz of Kalisz, castellans, and others worthy of trust, on their counsel and command we adjudged to Lord Kiełcz the custodian and to the church of Gniezno the estates of Stropiszyn and Panpycze to be possessed for ever, absolving him and the church of Gniezno from the suit and claim of Andrzej, heir of Trąbczyn, and of all his kin, who, setting him up before us, entrusted to him, in his name and theirs, the power to go to law for those estates; having heard his pleadings and those of the opposing party on both sides, we imposed perpetual silence concerning that estate on him and all his kin, granting to the said Lord Kiełcz and the church of Gniezno these our present letters, strengthened with our seal, concerning this judgment. Given and done at Pyzdry on the feast of St Brice, in the year of the Lord 1346, in the presence of the witnesses named above.

Troppessino, Stropiszyn. Panpycze, added in the margin: sive Kopydłowo, probably Pępocin; Kopydłowo lies 5 miles from the latter. Trampczino, Trąbczyn."""

E[11] = """Document no. 1739

Przecław, palatine of Kalisz, and the other judges, 27 May 1377, at Gniezno; declare before King Louis that the villages of Wrąbczynek and Adamierz, with the mill lying near the town of Pyzdry, have belonged to the monastery of Ląd from of old.

Original on parchment. On strips of parchment 6 small round seals are hung. The first is lost; the second is wholly worn; the third shows on a triangular shield the arms of the Poraj family, legend: S. Iohannis d. Nancowo; the fourth shows on a triangular shield the arms of the Zaremba family, legend: ...crol...; the fifth has its impression chipped; the sixth shows on a triangular shield the arms of the Doliwa family, legend worn.

Warsaw. Collections of the Rusiecki family. Diplomata.

To the magnificent prince Louis, King of Hungary and of Poland, Przecław the palatine, Tomisław the judge, Jan the castellan, Mikołaj the under-chamberlain of Kalisz, Wawrzyniec, castellan of Poznań, and Janusz, castellan of Biechowo, their most humble service at every pleasure of his commands. Before your royal majesty and all and each who are present we acknowledge, publicly professing: that little Wrąbczyn with the village of Nadamirza adjoining it, lying near Pyzdry, with all its uses, namely fields, plains, meadows, waters, woods, forests and the lake called Prziborowo, and also the mill lying below the town of Pyzdry, is of the property of the monastery of Ląd of the Cistercian Order; and we declare openly and publicly that this same village, namely little Wrąbczyn with its adjoining village, has belonged from of old, with full and entire lordship and all rights, revenues, fruits and incomes whatsoever, always to the said monastery of Ląd as its true property, as it does now. And in greater evidence of this our declaration we have had the present letters strengthened with our seals. Done and given at Gniezno on the eve of Corpus Christi, in the year of the Lord one thousand three hundred and seventy-seven, we being then gathered there for the general courts.

Behoviensis, of Biechowo. Parvum Wronczino, Wrąbczynek. Nadamirza, Adamierz. Lacus Prziborowo, today a marsh lying north of Wrąbczynek. Landensis, of Ląd."""

E[12] = """Document no. 283

Poznań, 31 January 1384

Mikołaj of Wenecja, judge of Kalisz, adjudges Wrąbczyn(ek) to the Cistercian monastery at Ląd.

Original: Kraków, WAP. D. Dep. 344. Parchment 229–219 × 130–126 + 23 mm. Three holes in the right margin, the bottom frayed. Slits from the strip for hanging the seal. On the back notes on the content and shelfmarks (14th–18th century).

In the name of the Lord, amen. We, Mikołaj, judge of Kalisz¹, make known to all whom it concerns | that at Poznań on the Sunday before the Purification of the glorious Virgin Mary, in the year of the Lord one thousand | three hundred and eighty-four, there came before us Piotrko, heir of Łęg², also | on behalf of his uterine brother, on the one part, and the religious man Rudger, key-keeper and procurator of the brothers and convent of the mo- nastery of Ląd³, on the other, in the presence of the reverend father in Christ Lord Jan, Duke of Opole and Bishop of Poznań⁴, and of the strenuous knights and nobles: Pielgrzym, captain of Poland⁵, Dzierżek, castellan of Santok⁶, Franczek the cupbearer⁷, Stefan the under-cupbearer of Poznań⁸, and of the honourable men: Wojciech, archdeacon of Poznań⁹, Mikołaj, cantor of Poznań¹⁰; and on the peremptory term set for them they went to law before us for the estate called Vronczino¹¹. And we, weighing their causes well on the information of the lords already written and of other elders of the land, adjudged that the said Rudger the key-keeper, in the name of the whole convent of the said monastery, should be given the investiture of the said village, and have adjudged it by these presents, not removing Piotrko or his brother from acting at law against the said village. Provided that they may try for the said village by law, doing no violence to the said monastery. In witness of which we have had our seal hung on these presents. Given and done at Poznań on the day, in the year and at the place above.

¹Mikołaj of Wenecja, see above, no. 228/23.

²Piotr of Łęg near Śrem), most probably the same as the later steward of Kalisz 1392, then deputy judge of Poznań 1393–1398 (Spisy wlkp.; SHGWlkp.). His brother not identified.

³The key-keeper of Ląd, Rudger, not otherwise known.

⁴Jan Kropidło, see above, no. 282/1.

⁵Peregryn, Pielgrzym, of Węgleszyn (in the land of Sandomierz), of the Jelita arms, under-chamberlain of Sandomierz 1379–1400, captain of Greater Poland 1383–1387 (PSB XXV, pp. 602–603).

⁶Dziersław, Dzierżek, Grochola of Ostroróg, of the Nałęcz arms, castellan of Santok 1383–1387 (Spisy wlkp.).

⁷Franciszek of Cerekwica, see above, no. 190/1.

⁸Stefan the under-cupbearer occurs only here; most probably, though, this is the steward of Poznań 1376–1399, Stefan of Trląg and Głogowiec (see above, no. 258/2).

⁹Wojciech of Będlewo, see above, no. 227/17.

¹⁰Mikołaj, cantor of Poznań, confirmed 1380–1384 (here), probably the deputy of (or the same as) Mikołaj Strosberg (see above, no. 250/4).

¹¹Wrąbczyn, just west of Zagórów, or Wrąbczynek (west of Wrąbczyn): in both these settlements, called Wronczyn (Little and Great), the monastery of Ląd later had its shares."""

E[13] = """Document no. 2014

Dobrogost, Archbishop of the holy church of Gniezno, 13 October 1399, in the stone room of the monastery of Ląd; adjudges the sheaf tithe of the village of Trąbczyn to the monastery of Ląd against the claim of Jan, parish priest of Trąbczyn.

Włocławek. Liber privilegiorum of the monastery of Ląd, p. 181.

In the name of the Lord, amen. We, Dobrogost, by the grace of God and of the Apostolic See Archbishop of the holy church of Gniezno, make known to all whom it concerns who will have knowledge of these presents that, whereas a matter of dispute had arisen before us between the venerable man Lord Gerard, abbot, and the whole convent of the monastery of Ląd of the Cistercian Order, of our diocese of Gniezno, plaintiffs, on the one part, and the discreet man Jan, rector of the church in Trąbczyn, defendant, on the other, concerning the sheaf tithe of the fields there in Trąbczyn: we therefore, the said parties appearing in person before us, having heard the propositions of the parties and the proofs brought forward which the said parties were wont to use, having taken the counsel of men learned in the law and with the consent, will and assent of the parties themselves, simply and plainly, without the noise and form of a trial, all the windings of lawsuits cut off, having invoked the name of Christ and having Him alone before our eyes and fearing Him, sitting as a tribunal, by this our definitive sentence pronounce, decree and declare in writing: that the sheaf tithe of the fields in Trąbczyn has belonged and belongs, has pertained and pertains, to Lord Abbot Gerard and the said convent, imposing perpetual silence on Jan, the said parish priest of Trąbczyn, concerning that tithe. In testimony and fuller evidence of which we have ordered the present sentence of ours to be written and published by Jan, public notary, our own and the scribe of this cause, and to be strengthened by the hanging of our seal. Done and given in the year of the Lord 1399, in the seventh indiction, in the tenth year of the pontificate of the most holy father and lord in Christ, Lord Boniface the Ninth, Pope by the worthy providence of God, on Monday the thirteenth day of the month of October, in the stone room of the monastery of Ląd, in the presence of the honourable men, the lords Mikołaj Strozberg, provost, Jarosław, archdeacon of Gniezno, and Maciej, rector of the church of Błędów, witnesses to the foregoing.

And I, Jan son of Wojciech of Wielatowo, cleric of the diocese of Gniezno, public notary by imperial authority and scribe of the most reverend father the Lord Archbishop and of this cause, since I was present together with the witnesses named above at all and each of the foregoing while they were (being done) as is set out above, and saw and heard all and each of them being done so, and, being busy with other arduous affairs, had them written by another faithful notary and brought into this public form, at the command of the said Lord Archbishop with the hanging of his seal I have subscribed here and signed it with my usual sign and name, in faith and testimony of the foregoing.

Landensis, of Ląd. Trambczyno, Trąbczyn. Blandowo, Błędów. Wielathow, Wielatowo."""

E[14] = """Document no. 117

1407, 19 November, at Niepołomice.

Władysław II, King of Poland, gives the monastery of Ląd the power to found a town in the village of Zagórów.

In the name of the Lord, amen. For the perpetual memory of the matter. The common good is well served, and the government of the realm is ordered with pleasing moderation, when among the other offices of rule granted from above to princes increase is ordained for each of the parts of the realm, and their conditions are changed both in kind and in particular, in public as well as in private. Therefore we, Władysław, by the grace of God King of Poland, and also supreme prince of the lands of Kraków, Sandomierz, Sieradz, Łęczyca, Kujawy and Lithuania, lord and heir of Pomerania and Ruthenia, etc., make known by the tenor of these presents to all and each, present as well as to come, whom it concerns, that, having had diligent regard to the honesty of praiseworthy life and the constancy of devotion of the religious man Gerhard, abbot, together with the brothers of his convent in Ląd, of the Cistercian Order, by which they have very greatly deserved to be commended in the sight of our majesty, at the urgent and humble requests of the said abbot and convent, wishing (to make) the condition of that convent better and to increase its profits, we give full power and grant every faculty by the tenor of these presents to found a town in their village called Zagórów,¹⁾ lying in our land of Greater Poland and in the district of Konin, and to make it to last for ever: giving and granting to the same town Sogorow(s) all the civic rights which the other towns of our realm enjoy in every way. And so that the said town of Zagórów may the better be founded and become populous, we transfer it from Polish law to the German law which is called of Magdeburg, to last for ever; removing there all Polish rights, ways and customs whatsoever which were wont to disturb the said German law of Magdeburg and might in any way hinder it. We further exempt, absolve and for ever free the advocate, burghers and inhabitants of that town from all jurisdiction and power of all the palatines, castellans, judges, under-judges and any officials (and) servants of our realm, so that they shall in no way have to answer when summoned before them or any of them in causes great or small, nor be bound to pay any penalties; but the townsmen and inhabitants of that town shall answer only before their advocate for the time being, and the advocate before his abbot, or, if the abbot is negligent and remiss in doing justice, then before us or our general assembly, when he has been called and summoned by our letter sealed with our seal, and not otherwise than by his German law of Magdeburg, to those who complain of him; and in criminal and capital causes, such as theft, blood, homicide, maiming of limbs and arson, and any other enormous offences, we give full power and grant by the tenor of these presents every faculty to the advocate of that town for the time being to judge, sentence, correct, punish, chastise and condemn within its bounds and limits, as the German law itself demands and requires in all its points, conditions and clauses, our royal rights however always saved in all things. We also establish in the same town of Zagórów a weekly market day on every Wednesday, to be held for ever without prejudice to the other towns; at which market it shall be lawful and free for all and each merchant and any men to sell, buy, exchange and carry on any trade. In witness of these letters, to which our seal is hung. Given at Niepołomice on the very day of St Elizabeth the widow, in the year of the Lord 1407. In the presence of the reverend fathers in Christ and lords: Mikołaj, Archbishop of the holy church of Gniezno, Wojciech, Bishop of Poznań, Jan of Tarnów, castellan and captain of Kraków, Piotr Kmita of Kraków, Jan Ligęza of Łęczyca, Jakusz of Koniecpol of Sieradz, palatines; our faithful beloved knights and very many others worthy of trust. On the report of the venerable lord(s) vice-chancellor of the Kingdom of Poland, etc.

From the Cologne copy-book of the privileges of Ląd, fol. 3.

¹⁾Zagorów, a town in the voivodeship of Kalisz, district of Konin."""
